  "html": "<div id=\"questionExplanation-148396\" class=\"questionExplanation\" style=\"display: block;\"><p>We can use the discrete uniform distribution to model the outcome of a single event when the support <mjx-container class=\"MathJax CtxtMenu_Attached_0\" jax=\"CHTML\" tabindex=\"0\" ctxtmenu_counter=\"29\" style=\"font-size: 113.1%; position: relative;\"><mjx-math class=\"MJX-TEX\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><mjx-mi class=\"mjx-i\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c1D446 TEX-I\"></mjx-c></mjx-mi></mjx-math><mjx-assistive-mml unselectable=\"on\" display=\"inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>S</mi></math></mjx-assistive-mml></mjx-container> is finite, and each <mjx-container class=\"MathJax CtxtMenu_Attached_0\" jax=\"CHTML\" tabindex=\"0\" ctxtmenu_counter=\"30\" style=\"font-size: 113.1%; position: relative;\"><mjx-math class=\"MJX-TEX\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><mjx-mi class=\"mjx-i\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c1D465 TEX-I\"></mjx-c></mjx-mi><mjx-mo class=\"mjx-n\" space=\"4\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c2208\"></mjx-c></mjx-mo><mjx-mi class=\"mjx-i\" space=\"4\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c1D446 TEX-I\"></mjx-c></mjx-mi></mjx-math><mjx-assistive-mml unselectable=\"on\" display=\"inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></math></mjx-assistive-mml></mjx-container> is equally likely.</p>\n<p>With that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Random variable I cannot be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. This is because the number of cars can be any positive integer. Moreover, each number of cars is not equally likely.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p>Random variable II can be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. This is because there are a finite number of letters in the alphabet, and since the selection is random, each letter has an equal probability of being selected.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p>Random variable III cannot be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. This is because the amount of time between events is a real number, so the support is not finite.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Therefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"</p>\n</div>",
